      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the data supporting the approval of selexipag and discuss its potential place in therapy for managing pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DATA SOURCES",
          "content": "A systematic review of the literature for all relevant articles was performed through January 16, 2017, using MEDLINE and SCOPUS. A manual search of references from reports of clinical trials, review articles, and recent conference abstracts was performed to identify additional relevant studies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY SELECTION AND DATA EXTRACTION",
          "content": "Eligible citations included in vitro or in vivo evaluations of selexipag, with no restrictions on patient population or indication. Data related to the patient populations and outcomes of interest were extracted from each citation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "DATA SYNTHESIS",
          "content": "Single phase II and phase III trials have been published evaluating selexipag in patients with PAH. In 43 patients, the phase II trial showed that selexipag significantly reduced pulmonary vascular resistance by 30% versus placebo ( P = 0.0045) and improved 6-minute walk distance by 24 m ( P < 0.05). The larger phase III trial enrolled 1156 patients with PAH, showing that selexipag lowered the incidence of death or PAH-related complications by 40% versus placebo ( P < 0.001). Selexipag also improved 6-minute walk distance and lowered hospitalization risk. Common adverse events included headache, diarrhea, nausea, and jaw pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The specific role of selexipag for managing PAH patients is unclear because of its modest efficacy, lack of mortality reduction, and cost similar to intravenous prostacyclins. Additional clinical trials exploring combination therapy as well as its role in other types of pulmonary hypertension are needed."
        }
      ]},
